<speak>
<p>Witness. Field Season One. Chapter Four: Fourteen Founders. The red wolf.</p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>Every wild red wolf on Earth — every single one — lives in one green corner of eastern North Carolina, on a peninsula of swamp and farm fields. There are roughly two dozen of them. And every red wolf alive, wild or captive, descends from just fourteen animals: the founders, caught in the late 1970s, when catching them all was the only way left to save them.</p>
<p>The red wolf is smaller than its gray cousin — long-legged, cinnamon behind the ears — and it lives the way wolves actually live. Not as a horde, but as a family. A mother and father and their kids. This is the story of a species that has already died out once, came back, nearly died again — and this spring, filled four dens with pups.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The past. The wolf we removed, then missed.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>For two centuries, the American South ran a campaign against its predators — bounties, traps, poison — while the forests themselves were cleared. The red wolf's range collapsed to the Gulf Coast of Texas and Louisiana. And as the wolves grew rare, coyotes moved into the emptiness and began absorbing them, litter by mixed litter.</p>
<p>By the 1970s the species was dissolving. So the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service did something desperate: it caught every red wolf it could find, and in 1980, declared the species extinct in the wild. Seventeen animals were captured. Fourteen proved to be pure red wolves. Those fourteen became the ancestors of every red wolf that has lived since.</p>
<p>Seven years later came the redemption. In 1987, four pairs were released at Alligator River National Wildlife Refuge in North Carolina — the first time anywhere on Earth that a carnivore declared extinct in the wild was returned to it. The first wild litter arrived within a year. By the late two-thousands, around a hundred and twenty red wolves ran wild.</p>
<p>Then it unraveled. Wolves shot in the confusion of coyote hunting. Packs fractured. Releases suspended in 2015. And by 2020, only about seven known wolves remained.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The present. The second second-chance.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>In January 2021, a federal court ruled the no-release policy unlawful and ordered the wolves' keepers back to work. A settlement locked in eight years of annual release plans. The recovery is now visibly, carefully, breathing again. This spring — 2026 — four wild litters were born, twelve to sixteen pups surviving their first months, one litter on a private landowner's property enrolled in a coexistence program. About two hundred and eighty more red wolves stand ready in breeding centers across the country.</p>
<p>The program's inventiveness is quietly moving. Biologists sterilize territorial coyotes and release them as placeholders — they hold the ground without breeding, until a wolf family displaces them. In April 2025, for the first time ever, a captive-born pup was slipped into a wild litter. And the wild mother raised it as her own.</p>
<p>The deadliest remaining enemy is ordinary: a highway. Vehicle strikes on Highway 64 are now the leading cause of death — eight wolves since 2020, including a father whose five pups did not survive his loss.</p>
<p>What is known: all wild red wolves live in that one five-county recovery area. The species descends from fourteen founders. And the collapse to about seven has turned back to roughly two dozen, plus this spring's pups.</p>
<p>What is unknown: the true count — only collared wolves are "known," so every number is a floor. And the wolf's very origins are honestly debated: its genome sits close to the coyote's, though a National Academies review affirmed it as a distinct species worth saving. The debate is real. The animal in the den, raising pups in the world's last red wolf territory, is not.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The future. What would improve its odds.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>One. Build the underpasses. A twenty-five-million-dollar federal grant will put eleven wildlife crossings and fencing under Highway 64 — turning the deadliest road into a doorway.</p>
<p>Two. Keep the pipeline flowing. The settlement guarantees annual releases; thirty-three captive breeding pairs were matched this season, and cross-fostering now moves genes both ways between pens and dens.</p>
<p>Three. Make neighbors into partners. Coexistence programs pay and support the landowners whose fields the wolves share. This spring's private-land litter is what that looks like when it works.</p>
<p>Four. Grow beyond one corner. The official recovery plan calls for three wild populations — seven hundred and forty wolves — a fifty-year project. And in the coyotes of the Gulf Coast, scientists have found ghost red wolf genes: a possible reservoir of lost diversity, for a species built on fourteen ancestors.</p>
<p>As the program's coordinator put it: we cannot recover this species on our own. It's the relationships, the trust, the collaboration, that really determine what is achievable. The red wolf has now been given a second chance, twice. This spring, four dens said it was worth it.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>One question to carry with you. The red wolf exists because people admitted a mistake, and then spent fifty years repairing it — and the repair is still not finished. What mistake would you be willing to spend that long making right?</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>Every claim in this chapter is backed by a dated source, listed in the chapter text. This narration uses a synthetic voice. Thank you for witnessing.</p>
</speak>
